{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"submission\": \"submission.json\",\n  \"summary\": \"Submitted a small sourced clarification that closes the residual the newly mapped offline-recovery behavior opened.\\n\\nContext from the index: three findings are confirmed_in_scope, the restart check is accepted as a bounded negative, map v9 is the accepted basis, audit units are exhausted, and pending_work is empty.\\n\\nProduct contents (draft-relative submission.json with the updated map.json and one newly acquired source range SR-RESTORE-SNAP = api.go 631-675):\\n\\nReading the startup installation shows that a recovered snapshot is installed with the same index for both configurations — the snapshot's configuration and configuration index are used to set the committed configuration and then the latest configuration — so a node restarted after offline recovery begins with latestIndex equal to committedIndex and the one-uncommitted-configuration gate is not confused by the forced configuration index. That replaces b-offline-recovery's open interaction question with the sourced relationship, while keeping the narrower residual that a caller fencing a later change on the recovered index could be misled, because that index is a forced value rather than the index of a real entry. The map change carries a preserves statement, and no confirmed finding or saved candidate depends on the offline recovery path.\\n\\nThe feedback records the answered proposition and refreshes the full remaining frontier: that fencing question, the election-restriction chain, the FSM consistency question, the heartbeat term-regression pair and shutdown non-response, the pre-vote and TimeoutNow liveness residuals, and the two out-of-snapshot questions.\\n\\nPreflight `validate` returned valid=true with no diagnostics. Acceptance and the final map version remain with the controller.\"\n}"}}
